9:Individuals at Clinical High Risk for Psychosis (CHR-P) demonstrate heterogeneity in clinical pro /uniFB01 les and outcome features. However, the extent of neuroanatomical heterogeneity in the CHR-P state is largely undetermined. We aimed to quantify the neuroanatomical heterogeneity in structural magnetic resonance imaging measures of cortical surface area (SA), cortical thickness (CT), subcortical volume (SV), and intracranial volume (ICV) in CHR-P individuals compared with healthy controls (HC), and in relation to subsequent transition to a /uniFB01 rst episode of psychosis. The ENIGMA CHR-P consortium applied a harmonised analysis to neuroimaging data across 29 international sites, including 1579 CHR-P individuals and 1243 HC, offering the largest pooled CHR-P neuroimaging dataset to date. Regional heterogeneity was indexed with the Variability Ratio (VR) and Coef /uniFB01 cient of Variation (CV) ratio applied at the group level. Personalised estimates of heterogeneity of SA, CT and SV brain pro /uniFB01 les were indexed with the novel Person-Based Similarity Index (PBSI), with two complementary applications. First, to assess the extent of within-diagnosis similarity or divergence of neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les between individuals. Second, using a normative modelling approach, to assess the ' normativeness ' of neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les in individuals at CHR-P. CHR-P individuals demonstrated no greater regional heterogeneity after applying FDR corrections. However, PBSI scores indicated signi /uniFB01 cantly greater neuroanatomical divergence in global SA, CT and SV pro /uniFB01 les in CHR-P individuals compared with HC. Normative PBSI analysis identi /uniFB01 ed 11 CHR-P individuals (0.70%) with marked deviation (&gt;1.5 SD) in SA, 118 (7.47%) in CT and 161 (10.20%) in SV. Psychosis transition was not signi /uniFB01 cantly associated with any measure of heterogeneity. Overall, our examination of neuroanatomical heterogeneity within the CHR-P state indicated greater divergence in neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les at an individual level, irrespective of psychosis conversion. Further largescale investigations are required of those who demonstrate marked deviation.
